 To find all values of $k$ for which the system has a non-trivial solution, we need to determine when the determinant of the coefficient matrix equals zero. The determinant is given by:

\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix}
1 & k & -1 \\
k & -1 & -1 \\
1 & 1 & -k
\end{vmatrix} &= 0
\end{align*}

Expanding the determinant along the first row, we get:

\begin{align*}
-1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} -1 & -1 \\ 1 & -k \end{vmatrix} - k \cdot \begin{vmatrix} k & -1 \\ 1 & -k \end{vmatrix} - 1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} k & -1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} &= 0
\end{align*}

Simplifying the determinants, we obtain:

\begin{align*}
-1 \cdot (-k + 1) - k \cdot (k^2 + 1) - 1 \cdot (k + 1) &= 0 \\
k - 1 - k^3 - k - k - 1 &= 0 \\
k^3 + 2k - 1 &= 0
\end{align*}

Factoring the cubic equation, we find:

\begin{align*}
(k - 1)(k^2 + k + 1) &= 0
\end{align*}

Therefore, the values of $k$ for which the system has a non-trivial solution are $k = 1$ and the roots of the quadratic equation $k^2 + k + 1 = 0$.

